  "headline": "Growth scare / flight to quality (mixed confidence).",
  "body": "WHAT MOVED\n\nThe S&P 500 fell 1.92 percent while the five-year Treasury yield dropped 5.0 basis points, with the VIX rising 3.61 points. Breakeven inflation expectations on the ten-year fell 4.0 basis points, copper slid 1.59 percent, and WTI crude lost 4.01 percent, while credit excess spreads tightened 0.43 percent.\n\nLIKELY DRIVER\n\nThe pattern is consistent with a growth scare accompanied by flight to quality. The decline in equities alongside a rally in intermediate-duration Treasuries, combined with falling breakeven inflation and weakness in cyclical commodities like copper and oil, would be typical of a session where investors repriced downward growth expectations. The rise in the VIX to accompany equity losses and the tightening of credit excess spreads reinforce the risk-off tone.\n\nWHAT TO WATCH\n\nConfirmation of this regime would require sustained pressure on cyclical assets and further Treasury strength, though notably gold fell 0.26 percent rather than rallying, which conflicts with the growth-scare narrative and warrants monitoring as either a measurement lag or a signal that the underlying driver differs from the pattern suggests.",
